<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Kingdoms of Korea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE–668</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> were Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla, which competed for control of the Korean peninsula and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> from about the first century BCE until the seventh century CE. The Gaya confederacy also existed in the south until Silla absorbed it in 562. In alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, Silla destroyed Baekje in 660 and Goguryeo in 668.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-of-korea.jpg" alt="Three Kingdoms of Korea" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5th_century_Korea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
